        "heading": "Querying for Triage: KQL and SPL Basics",
        "content": "You cannot triage what you cannot query. The good news is that a very small set of operators covers the vast majority of real triage work. You do not need to be a query-language wizard; you need fluency in about six verbs and the instinct to reach for them in the right order: narrow the time range, filter to what matters, aggregate to see the shape, and project the columns you care about.\n\n### Filtering: where and filter\n\nEvery investigation starts by narrowing. In KQL the operator is where; in SPL you filter with search terms directly or with the where command for expressions. You almost always narrow by time first — earliest=-24h in SPL, TimeGenerated > ago(24h) in KQL — because scanning less data is faster and cheaper. Then you filter by the entity under investigation: a user, an IP, a host. \"Show me every sign-in for this UPN in the last day\" is the single most common triage query in existence, and it is one filter on one field.\n\n### Aggregating: stats and summarize\n\nRaw events overwhelm you; aggregation reveals patterns. SPL uses stats; KQL uses summarize, and they are conceptually identical. Both take a set of aggregation functions — count, dcount (distinct count), sum, min, max, values or make_set — and a by clause listing the fields to group on. The mental model is a pivot table. \"Count failed logons by source IP\" instantly shows you which IP is loudest. \"Distinct-count of destination hosts by source IP\" surfaces the box that touched fifty servers. Aggregation is where noise becomes signal.\n\n### Counting and Shaping: count and project\n\ncount is both an aggregation function and, in KQL, a standalone operator that just returns how many rows matched — useful for a quick \"did anything happen at all\" check. project in KQL (table in SPL) selects and orders the columns you want to see, which turns a wall of forty fields into the five that matter for this question. A well-shaped result — time, user, IP, result, location — reads at a glance.\n\n### Putting It Together\n\nThe rhythm of triage is always the same: filter down to the entity and time window, summarize to see the behavior's shape, then project the fields that answer your question, and sort so the interesting rows float to the top. Master that rhythm and you can triage in any SIEM after a day of learning its dialect, because the concepts transfer even when the syntax does not. The example below is the workhorse triage query: given a suspicious account, what does its authentication history actually look like — how many successes, how many failures, from how many IPs and countries, and when did the first and last events happen.",
        "codeExample": "// SPL (Splunk): triage an account's authentication history from a suspicious sign-in\n// Filter -> aggregate -> shape. Swap the UPN for the account under investigation.\nindex=azuread sourcetype=azure:aad:signin UserPrincipalName=\"j.rivera@contoso.com\" earliest=-7d\n| stats count as total,\n        count(eval(ResultType=0)) as successes,\n        count(eval(ResultType!=0)) as failures,\n        dc(IPAddress) as distinct_ips,\n        dc(Location) as distinct_countries,\n        values(Location) as countries,\n        min(_time) as first_seen,\n        max(_time) as last_seen\n        by UserPrincipalName\n| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen,\"%F %T\"), last_seen=strftime(last_seen,\"%F %T\")\n| table UserPrincipalName total successes failures distinct_ips distinct_countries countries first_seen last_seen\n\n// KQL equivalent (Microsoft Sentinel), same logic in the other dialect:\n// SigninLogs\n// | where TimeGenerated > ago(7d) and UserPrincipalName == \"j.rivera@contoso.com\"\n// | summarize total=count(),\n//             successes=countif(ResultType==0),\n//             failures=countif(ResultType!=0),\n//             distinct_ips=dcount(IPAddress),\n//             countries=make_set(Location,10),\n//             first_seen=min(TimeGenerated), last_seen=max(TimeGenerated)\n//           by UserPrincipalName",
        "image": {
          "src": "/lesson-images/siem/kql-vs-spl.svg",
          "alt": "The same account-authentication triage query written side by side in KQL and SPL, filtering to the last 24 hours and one user, aggregating counts of total and failed sign-ins by IP address, and projecting the result columns. Below it a five-row operator translation table maps time filtering, field filtering, aggregation, column selection, and distinct counting between the two languages.",
          "caption": "Same query, two dialects — the rhythm of filter, aggregate, shape never changes.",
          "credit": "Figure authored for this course."
        }
