      {
        "heading": "Proactive Threat Hunting with EDR",
        "content": "Everything covered so far in this lesson is **reactive**: an alert fires, and you investigate it. Threat hunting is the other half of the EDR discipline — **proactively searching raw telemetry for malicious activity that never triggered any alert at all**, because no rule was written for it, the behavior fell just under a threshold, or the technique is genuinely new. Hunting is hypothesis-driven, not alert-driven, and it is one of the highest-leverage skills an EDR-equipped analyst can grow into.\n\n### Reactive Triage vs Proactive Hunting\n\nThe distinction matters because the starting point is inverted. Triage begins with an alert and works outward to build context; hunting begins with a **hypothesis** — a specific, falsifiable guess about attacker behavior — and works through raw, unfiltered telemetry to prove or disprove it, regardless of whether anything ever fired. A hunter is explicitly looking in the space where detection coverage is weakest: exactly the red cells on the ATT&CK Navigator coverage layer from earlier in this course, or techniques the organization knows it has only partial visibility into.\n\n### The Hunting Loop\n\nA disciplined hunt follows a repeatable loop. First, form a **hypothesis** grounded in threat intelligence, a recent incident, or an ATT&CK technique with known coverage gaps — for example, \"are any LOLBins on our servers spawning outbound network connections that our current rules do not flag?\" Second, **query raw telemetry** directly — not the alert queue, but the underlying process, network, file, and registry event tables — because the entire point is to look at activity the detection layer never surfaced. Third, **analyze** the results for outliers: rare parent-child pairs, unusual command-line patterns, infrequent destinations. Fourth, **document findings** — either a confirmed benign explanation, a genuine finding requiring escalation, or, most valuably, a **gap** that becomes a new detection rule so the next occurrence is caught automatically instead of requiring another manual hunt.\n\n### Where Hunters Query\n\nEach vendor exposes a path to raw telemetry beyond the pre-built detection queue: CrowdStrike Falcon's Event Search and Fusion workflows, Defender for Endpoint's Advanced Hunting (the same DeviceProcessEvents/DeviceNetworkEvents KQL tables from earlier in this lesson, but queried freeform rather than through a saved analytics rule), and SentinelOne's Deep Visibility / PowerQuery. The tables are frequently the *same* ones a scheduled detection rule reads — the difference is that hunting queries are exploratory and frequency-based rather than a fixed condition waiting to match.\n\n### A Frequency-Based Hunting Technique\n\nOne of the most productive hunting patterns is **frequency analysis** — also called \"stack counting\": rather than searching for a known-bad indicator, you count how often a given parent-child process pair, command-line pattern, or destination occurs across the fleet, and inspect the *rarest* occurrences first. Malicious activity is very often the outlier in an otherwise repetitive, IT-standardized environment — a parent-child pair that occurred once across ten thousand endpoints is far more interesting than one that occurred ten thousand times, because attackers rarely replicate their exact tooling fleet-wide the way software deployment tools do.\n\n| Aspect | Reactive Triage | Proactive Hunting |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Starting point | An alert that already fired | A hypothesis, with no alert required |\n| Data queried | The flagged event and its immediate context | Raw, unfiltered telemetry across the fleet or time window |\n| Goal | Disposition this specific alert | Find what detection never surfaced; produce a new rule or a confirmed gap |\n| Typical technique | Filter-aggregate-shape on a known entity | Frequency analysis / stack counting to surface rare outliers |",
        "codeExample": "// KQL hunting query (Defender for Endpoint Advanced Hunting):\n// Frequency analysis / stack counting -- surface the RAREST parent-child process\n// pairs fleet-wide over the last 7 days. No alert required to start this hunt.\nDeviceProcessEvents\n| where Timestamp > ago(7d)\n| where InitiatingProcessFileName in~ (\"WINWORD.EXE\", \"EXCEL.EXE\", \"OUTLOOK.EXE\")\n| summarize Occurrences = count(), Hosts = dcount(DeviceName)\n          by InitiatingProcessFileName, FileName, tostring(ProcessCommandLine)\n| where Occurrences <= 3                       // the rare, low-frequency outliers\n| sort by Occurrences asc\n| project InitiatingProcessFileName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, Occurrences, Hosts\n\n// Hypothesis this hunt tests: \"is Office spawning an uncommon child process\n// somewhere in the fleet that our detection rules do not already flag?\"\n// A result appearing on exactly ONE host, with an unusual command line,\n// is exactly the kind of outlier stack-counting is designed to surface --\n// and if confirmed malicious, becomes a new detection rule, not a one-off close."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Independent EDR Testing: The MITRE ATT&CK Evaluations",
        "content": "Every EDR vendor claims strong detection coverage in its marketing. The **MITRE ATT&CK Evaluations** exist to test that claim independently: an annual, publicly published exercise, run since 2018, in which MITRE emulates a real, named adversary's actual tactics, techniques, and procedures — past rounds have emulated groups such as APT29, Carbanak and FIN7, and Wizard Spider with Sandworm — against each participating vendor's product in a controlled environment, and publishes the detailed, step-by-step results for anyone to read. Learning to read those results, rather than a vendor's summary slide, is a genuinely useful skill when your organization evaluates or tunes an EDR platform.\n\n### The Detection Categories\n\nThe evaluation does not simply say \"detected\" or \"missed.\" Each individual attack step is scored into one of several precise categories, and the categories matter enormously because they describe *how well* the tool understood what happened, not just whether something lit up: **Not Applicable** — the vendor's sensor is not deployed on that platform at all, so there is no visibility to evaluate; **None** — the sensor was present but no data at all was captured for that step; **Telemetry** — the tool recorded that the event happened, but did not assess or flag it as malicious; **General (Behavior)** — the tool flagged the activity as suspicious, without correctly identifying the specific tactic or technique involved; and **Tactic/Technique** — the tool correctly, specifically identified the actual tactic or technique being performed, such as naming credential dumping by name rather than a vague \"suspicious activity\" flag.\n\n### Why the Categories Matter More Than a Headline Number\n\nThis granularity is the entire point, and it is exactly what a marketing claim of \"100% detection\" tends to obscure. A vendor can technically \"detect\" every single step of an emulation and still have scored almost entirely in the **Telemetry** category — meaning the data was recorded, but nothing meaningfully alerted an analyst or named the technique. That is a very different product experience than a vendor whose steps mostly land in the **Technique** category, where the tool is actively naming what it saw. Reading the step-by-step results — not just an aggregate percentage — tells you whether a tool's strength is *seeing* everything (useful for hunting and forensics, per the previous section) or *naming* everything precisely (useful for fast, low-effort triage), and those are genuinely different strengths that suit different SOC maturity levels.\n\n### Protection Tests, and How to Use the Results\n\nSeparately from the detection-category scoring, MITRE has also run **protection** evaluations, which test whether a product actively *blocks* an attack technique rather than only observing it — a distinct question from detection, mirroring the detect-only-versus-prevent distinction covered earlier in this lesson. For an analyst or a SOC evaluating tooling, the practical use of these public results is to cross-reference your own product's published evaluation against the techniques you most need covered (again, the same ATT&CK Navigator coverage-mapping exercise from the SIEM lesson, applied to your EDR specifically) — a vendor that scores well on Technique-level detection for credential access but weakly for defense evasion tells you precisely where to compensate with additional detection engineering or a complementary tool, rather than assuming the product is uniformly strong everywhere.\n\n| Detection Category | What It Means |\n| --- | --- |\n| Not Applicable | No sensor deployed on this platform — no visibility possible |\n| None | Sensor present, but no data captured for this step |\n| Telemetry | Event was recorded, but not assessed as malicious |\n| General (Behavior) | Flagged as suspicious, without naming the specific tactic/technique |\n| Tactic/Technique | Correctly and specifically identified the actual tactic or technique |",
        "codeExample": "// Illustrative excerpt of how a single emulated attack step is scored and\n// reported in a MITRE ATT&CK Evaluation results record (format simplified for teaching)\n\n{\n  \"scenario\": \"Emulated adversary - Step 4.B\",\n  \"technique\": \"T1003.001\",              // OS Credential Dumping: LSASS Memory\n  \"tactic\": \"Credential Access\",\n  \"vendor_result\": {\n    \"detection_category\": \"Telemetry\",   // saw LSASS access, did NOT flag it as malicious\n    \"detection_category_alt\": \"General\",  // some vendors ALSO flagged it generically\n    \"protection_result\": \"Not Blocked\"    // the credential dump was NOT prevented\n  }\n}\n\n// Reading this record correctly:\n//   - The tool is NOT blind (it is not \"None\") -- LSASS access was recorded.\n//   - But it did not name the technique -- an analyst reviewing this alone\n//     would see raw telemetry, not a clear \"credential dumping\" alert.\n//   - And the attack was not blocked, so it still executed.\n// A single headline \"92% detection\" hides ALL of this nuance --\n// which is exactly why reading the per-step category matters more than the total."
      }
